  "content": {
    "id": "p23-q15-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "15",
    "prompt": "A right-angled triangle has a base of length $3$ and a height of $2$. An infinite set of squares are arranged along the base as shown in the diagram below.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [
      {
        "id": "p23-q15-diagram",
        "role": "question",
        "format": "tikz",
        "code": "% mathsmap-diagram-colours {\"version\":1,\"kind\":\"geometry\",\"base\":[],\"semantic\":[{\"name\":\"squareFill\",\"hex\":\"D4E8FF\",\"reason\":\"identifies the squares whose total area is requested\"}],\"reference\":\"source PDF page 23, p23-q15-diagram\"}\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[x=2cm,y=2cm,line width=0.8pt,font=\\fontsize{10}{12}\\selectfont]\n\\special{dvisvgm:raw <metadata data-graph-strokes=\"1\"/>}\n\\definecolor{squareFill}{HTML}{D4E8FF}\n\\foreach \\k in {0,...,8}{\n  \\pgfmathsetmacro{\\rightedge}{3*pow(0.6,\\k)}\n  \\pgfmathsetmacro{\\side}{1.2*pow(0.6,\\k)}\n  \\pgfmathsetmacro{\\leftedge}{\\rightedge-\\side}\n  \\path[draw=black,fill=squareFill] (\\leftedge,0) rectangle (\\rightedge,\\side);\n}\n\\draw[black] (0,0)--(3,0)--(3,2)--cycle;\n\\path (0,0)--(3,0) node[midway,below] {$3$};\n\\path (3,0)--(3,2) node[midway,right] {$2$};\n\\end{tikzpicture}",
        "src": null,
        "widthMm": 70,
        "alt": "Mathematical diagram",
        "overlayOf": null,
        "transparent": false,
        "axes": null,
        "spec": {
          "sourcePage": 23,
          "sourceRegion": "Question 15, upper right column",
          "description": "Adjacent squares increase in size towards the vertical right side of a right-angled triangle. Each square rests on the base and its top-left corner touches the hypotenuse. Nine visible squares represent the infinite sequence.",
          "mathematics": "Triangle vertices are (0,0), (3,0), (3,2). For drawing only, square k has right edge 3(3/5)^k and side length (6/5)(3/5)^k. These derived values are not printed.",
          "answerVisibility": "Only the given triangle lengths 3 and 2 are labelled; square dimensions and area results remain hidden."
        },
        "derived": false,
        "reviewStatus": "needs-review"
      }
    ],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p23-q15-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Find the side length of the largest square.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\dfrac65$ units.",
          "worked": "Let $x$ be the side length of the largest square. The triangle above it is similar to the whole triangle.\n$$\\begin{align*}\n\\frac{x}{2-x}&=\\frac32\\\\\n2x&=3(2-x)\\\\\n2x&=6-3x\\\\\n5x&=6\\\\\nx&=\\frac65.\n\\end{align*}$$\nThe largest square has side length $\\dfrac65$ units.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 48
      },
      {
        "id": "p23-q15-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Find the total area of all the squares.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p23-q15-b-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "\\dfrac94",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " square units.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p23-q15-b-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Sum the geometric series of areas with first term ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "\\dfrac{36}{25}",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " and common ratio ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "\\dfrac9{25}",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ".",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "After removing the largest square, the remaining triangle on the left is similar to the original triangle. Its base is $3-\\dfrac65=\\dfrac95$, so successive square side lengths have ratio $\\dfrac{\\frac{9}{5}}{3}=\\dfrac35$.\n$$\\begin{align*}\na&=\\left(\\frac65\\right)^2\\\\\na&=\\frac{36}{25}\\\\\nr&=\\left(\\frac35\\right)^2\\\\\nr&=\\frac9{25}.\n\\end{align*}$$\nSince $|r|<1$, the total area is\n$$\\begin{align*}\nS&=\\frac{a}{1-r}\\\\\nS&=\\frac{36/25}{1-9/25}\\\\\nS&=\\frac94.\n\\end{align*}$$\nThe total area is $\\dfrac94$ square units.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 64
      }
    ]
  },
